7. Machine Learning: Training, Evaluation and Inference

Inspect all existing ML scripts before changing them.

Implement a repeatable pipeline for each supported task:

1. Validate the input dataset.
2. Check required columns and units.
3. Handle missing values and duplicates.
4. Verify chronological order for time-series tasks.
5. Prevent target leakage.
6. Split data appropriately.
7. Establish a baseline.
8. Train the model.
9. Evaluate on held-out data.
10. Save the complete preprocessing and model pipeline.
11. Record the model version, features, training period and metrics.
12. Load and validate the artifact during inference.

Where appropriate, persist preprocessing and the estimator together using a scikit-learn pipeline and joblib.

Never assume that a ".pkl" file exists merely because the application expects one.

Add checks for missing, corrupted or incompatible model artifacts.

Use reproducible random seeds where relevant, sensible training defaults and clear CLI help messages.

Document the data required to train each model. If the available dataset is insufficient, explain the limitation and provide a safe demonstration path without presenting demonstration results as validated predictions.

