14. Required Working Method and Final Deliverables

Follow this sequence:

Phase 1 — Audit: Inspect the complete repository, identify current functionality, reproduce failures and report a prioritized issue list.

Phase 2 — Plan: Propose a practical implementation order, identifying dependencies and avoiding unnecessary rewrites.

Phase 3 — UI and UX: Improve farmer and admin interfaces, shared components, responsive layouts, language support and status states.

Phase 4 — Integration: Connect the interface to Flask routes, services, database queries, data sources and ML inference.

Phase 5 — Data and ML: Validate data handling, train and evaluate models when suitable data exists, and integrate only compatible artifacts.

Phase 6 — Security: Apply authentication, authorization, validation, secret management and other relevant safeguards.

Phase 7 — Testing: Run tests, fix failures and verify critical workflows.

Phase 8 — Documentation: Update setup instructions, architecture, model details, limitations and deployment guidance.

At the end, provide:

1. A summary of the original issues found.
2. A list of files changed and why.
3. A list of completed features.
4. A list of features that still need credentials, real data or external services.
5. Test commands and actual test results.
6. Screenshots or a walkthrough of the farmer and admin portals, if the environment supports them.
7. Exact Windows/VS Code commands to run the final project.
8. Any remaining known issues and recommended next steps.

Important execution instruction: Do not merely return suggestions, mockups, code snippets or a plan. When repository editing tools are available, implement the changes directly in the existing project, run the application and tests, and deliver the updated project files. If a step cannot be completed because data, credentials, tools or permissions are unavailable, explain the limitation and complete all independent work that can be done safely.

Final quality goal: AgriShield should be a practical, transparent and trustworthy agricultural decision-support prototype that a farmer can understand, an administrator can operate, and a college evaluator can verify. Prioritize usefulness, correctness, accessibility and reliability over unnecessary features or decorative complexity.